## What does this PR do?

Fixes a CLI syntax error in `src/cli/index.js` caused by using `await import(...)` inside a non-async `SIGINT` callback.

## Type of change

- [x] Bug fix
- [ ] New feature
- [ ] Documentation update
- [ ] Refactoring
- [ ] New skill (`skills/*/SKILL.md`)
- [ ] Test fix
- [ ] Other

## Runtime context

- OS: Arch Linux
- Node.js: v20.20.2

## Problem

The CLI failed before startup with:

```bash
SyntaxError: Unexpected reserved word
```

The issue came from this pattern:

```js
process.on('SIGINT', () => {
  const { stopMonitor: stop } = await import('../analytics/reputation.js');
  stop(monitor.id);
  console.log(chalk.yellow('\nMonitor stopped.'));
  process.exit(0);
});
```

## Root cause

`await` was used inside a callback that was not marked `async`.

## Fix

Changed the handler to:

```js
process.on('SIGINT', async () => {
  const { stopMonitor: stop } = await import('../analytics/reputation.js');
  stop(monitor.id);
  console.log(chalk.yellow('\nMonitor stopped.'));
  process.exit(0);
});
```

## Validation

Verified locally with:

```bash
npm run cli -- --help
npm run cli -- tweets paoloanzn --limit 10
```

Both commands worked successfully after the patch.

Three pre-launch issues on mobile: Twitch iframe only filling ~half the player container, panel title briefly flashing a stale creator name on load, and white background visible while the offline player initializes.

## Name flicker
Removed the spreadsheet `status` column fallback (`"live"` / `"active"`) from the `communityLiveNow` filter. The panel title now only changes when `live-status.json` (Twitch Helix API, written by GitHub Actions) confirms a stream is live — stale spreadsheet data can no longer trigger a title update.

```js
// Before — spreadsheet status could override Twitch API result
serverLiveUsernames.has(c.twitch) || c.status === "live" || c.status === "active"

// After — server-confirmed only
serverLiveUsernames.has(c.twitch)
```

## Mobile iframe scaling
On `≤768px`, `#offline-player` now uses `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` + `flex: none` instead of `flex: 1` against the panel's `min-height`. This gives the container a concrete intrinsic height the iframe can fill. Also clears `.twitch-panel { min-height: 0 }` on mobile so the panel sizes to content.

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .twitch-panel        { width: 100% !important; min-height: 0; }
  #offline-player      { flex: none; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
  #offline-player iframe { width: 100% !important; height: 100% !important; }
}
```

## Background color
Added `background: #000000` to the `#offline-player` base rule to prevent the white-box flash during player initialization.

Both `style.css` and `style.min.css` (the file actually served) are updated.

`TcpStream::try_clone()` unconditionally returned `ENOTSUP` on thingos, making multi-threaded connection-sharing patterns impossible with standard library types.

## Changes

- **`patches/rust/95-net.patch`** (`sys/net/connection/thingos.rs`)
  - Added `const SYS_FS_DUP: u32 = 0x400C;` alongside the other VFS syscall constants
  - Replaced `TcpStream::duplicate()` stub with a real implementation: dups both `data_fd` and `ctl_fd` via `SYS_FS_DUP`, with proper cleanup if the second dup fails; constructs an independent `TcpStream` with fresh timeout/nonblocking state

```rust
pub fn duplicate(&self) -> io::Result<TcpStream> {
    let new_data_fd = {
        let ret = unsafe { raw_syscall6(SYS_FS_DUP, self.data_fd as usize, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) };
        decode_ret(ret).map(|fd| fd as i32)?
    };
    let new_ctl_fd = {
        let ret = unsafe { raw_syscall6(SYS_FS_DUP, self.ctl_fd as usize, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) };
        match decode_ret(ret).map(|fd| fd as i32) {
            Ok(fd) => fd,
            Err(e) => { let _ = vfs_close(new_data_fd); return Err(e); }
        }
    };
    Ok(TcpStream { id: self.id, data_fd: new_data_fd, ctl_fd: new_ctl_fd, peer_addr: self.peer_addr, ... })
}
```

Leverages the existing kernel-side `SYS_FS_DUP` handler (`FdTable::dup`) which shares the underlying `Arc<VfsNode>` — matching POSIX open-file-description semantics. Follows the same pattern already used by `File::duplicate()` in `70-fs.patch`.
